# Line-ending and binary handling.
#
# **Order matters: the last matching line wins.** Generic rules come first, fixture
# overrides last. Getting that backwards silently un-protects the fixtures, and the
# symptom is bytes changing on somebody else's checkout rather than an error here - so
# any edit to this file should be checked with `git check-attr text eol binary -- <path>`
# against one fixture and one source file (D208).

# --- source: LF everywhere, in the repository and in every working copy ----------
#
# Stated rather than left to whatever `core.autocrlf` a contributor happens to have.
# `eol=lf` rather than plain `text`, which would check out CRLF on Windows: this tree is
# LF on disk and half the tooling is a bash script run under Git Bash. A working copy that
# differs by platform is a diff appearing out of nowhere on somebody else's machine.
*.rs               text eol=lf diff=rust
*.toml             text eol=lf
*.lock             text eol=lf
*.md               text eol=lf
*.txt              text eol=lf
*.json             text eol=lf
*.yml              text eol=lf
*.sh               text eol=lf
*.html             text eol=lf
*.css              text eol=lf
# Extensionless and dotted files the patterns above cannot reach. `.githooks/*` in
# particular: a hook checked out with CRLF fails on a `
` in the shebang line.
.gitattributes     text eol=lf
.gitignore         text eol=lf
.githooks/*        text eol=lf
# The entry point has no extension, so none of the rules above reach it - it was the one
# script in the collection with no eol rule at all, and a bash script checked out with CRLF
# fails as `bad interpreter: /usr/bin/env bash^M`, which names the shell rather than the
# line endings. Every other repository covers it with a blanket `* text=auto eol=lf`.
bin/*              text eol=lf
LICENSE-*          text eol=lf

# --- binary, wherever it lives ---------------------------------------------------
#
# Never diffed, never translated. `.gcn` is guest shader bytecode and was the dangerous
# omission: ten binary fixtures git would have treated as text on a Windows clone, turning
# every 0x0A inside them into 0x0D 0x0A - corrupting the material the whole differential
# suite is built on, in a way that looks like a decoder bug.
*.gcn              binary
*.bin              binary
*.spv              binary
*.png              binary

# --- fixtures: git does not touch these bytes at all -----------------------------
#
# `-text` rather than `eol=lf`, which would still rewrite a fixture that legitimately
# contains CRLF - and a *line protocol* transcript is exactly the thing that might. These
# are compared byte-for-byte, so the correct instruction is "leave them alone", not
# "normalise them the way we normalise source".
#
# Unanchored, because there is no `tests/` at the repository root and there never has
# been: every fixture lives under `crates/<crate>/tests/`. This file used to say
# `tests/fixtures/**`, which is anchored, and therefore matched nothing at all.
**/tests/fixtures/**        -text
**/tests/captures/**        -text
**/tools/shader-fixtures/** -text
tests/fixtures/**           -text
